In the Gallup surveys, respondents answered every question themselves. In the Survation surveys, a parent first gave consent and reported the child's age, gender, region and the type of place they live in, before handing the device to the child, who answered the rest of the questionnaire. In both second waves, the providers first invited their own first-wave respondents back and then filled the sample with new respondents. Because few Gallup respondents returned, Gallup W2 added a separate sample of 22 to 26 year olds, which is why its age range is wider. Survation W2, in turn, also admitted 18 year olds outside its quota (25 respondents).

